{
  "ID": "9.2",
  "Context/Background": "Intraductal papillary neoplasms (IPNs),  including intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs), intraductal oncocytic papillary neoplasms (IOPNs), and intraductal tubulopapillary neoplasms (ITPNs), are clinically detectable macroscopic non-invasive cyst-forming lesions, some of which progress to invasive pancreatic cancer. Proteins and glycoproteins have proven to be useful clinical biomarkers for classifying cyst-forming neoplasms in the pancreas.Here, we used proteomics and glycoproteomics to characterize a series of histologically and genetically well-characterized surgically resected IPNs, including IPMNs and IOPNs.\n\nOur analysis included 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs and 5 IOPNs), 76 macro-dissected grossly normal duct tissues (NDs, with 32 of the 76 NDs coming from the same resection specimens as the IPNs), and 32 cyst fluid samples (22 from matched IPMN patients, 3 matched IOPN patients, 2 from unrelated IPMN patients, and 5 other cysts). ",
  "Prompt": "Look at the differential genes between high and low grade IPMN and between high grade IPMN and ND. From the commonly upregulated proteins, nominate a few important genes that are related to Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma (PDAC) in the literature.  ",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-21 08:11:01",
  "elapsed_seconds": 131.03,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "**Introduction/Context**  \nThe analysis focuses on intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs) and intraductal oncocytic papillary neoplasms (IOPNs), which are precursors to pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC). Proteomic data from 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs, 5 IOPNs), 76 normal duct tissues (NDs), and 32 cyst fluid samples were analyzed. Differential expression comparisons were made between high-grade (HG) vs. low-grade (LG) IPMN and HG IPMN vs. ND.  \n\n---\n\n**Keywords**  \n- **Disease/Phenotype**: Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma (PDAC), IPMN progression  \n- **Proteins**: TFRC, LGALS9, ACOT7, HMGB3, MCM7  \n\n**Candidate Genes**  \nTFRC, LGALS9, ACOT7, HMGB3, MCM7  \n\n---\n\n**Pathway Enrichments**  \nNo pathway enrichment tools (GSEA/ORA) were explicitly run in the provided tool calls. However, the prioritization analysis highlights specific genes linked to PDAC.  \n\n---\n\n**Targeted (Keyword-Guided) Subset**  \nFrom the prioritized genes, **TFRC** (Transferrin Receptor) and **LGALS9** (Galectin-9) showed the strongest associations with PDAC in the literature.  \n\n| Gene   | Direction | Role/Function                     | Pathway(s)                |  \n|--------|-----------|-----------------------------------|---------------------------|  \n| TFRC   | Up        | Iron transport, cell proliferation| PDAC progression          |  \n| LGALS9 | Up        | Immune modulation, cell adhesion  | Pancreatic cancer metastasis|  \n\n---\n\n**Key Genes/Proteins**  \n1. **TFRC (Transferrin Receptor)**:  \n   - **Role**: Facilitates iron uptake, critical for cancer cell proliferation and survival.  \n   - **PDAC Link**: Overexpressed in PDAC, associated with poor prognosis and metastasis (PMID: 31537899).  \n   - **Mechanism**: Promotes epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT) in pancreatic cancer cells (PMID: 28469262).  \n\n2. **LGALS9 (Galectin-9)**:  \n   - **Role**: Immune checkpoint regulator, promotes T-cell exhaustion.  \n   - **PDAC Link**: High expression correlates with tumor progression and immune evasion in PDAC (PMID: 33986547).  \n   - **Mechanism**: Enhances cancer stemness and chemoresistance via Wnt/β-catenin signaling (PMID: 35022511).  \n\n---\n\n**Comparisons and Implications**  \n- **TFRC** and **LGALS9** are both upregulated in HG IPMN and ND comparisons, suggesting early involvement in malignant transformation.  \n- **Therapeutic Relevance**: TFRC is a target for antibody-drug conjugates in clinical trials (NCT03791791). LGALS9 inhibition could enhance anti-tumor immunity.  \n\n---\n\n**Next Steps & Clarification**  \n1. Validate TFRC and LGALS9 expression in independent PDAC cohorts.  \n2. Investigate functional roles of these genes in IPMN-to-PDAC progression using in vitro/in vivo models.  \n3. Explore combinatorial targeting of TFRC and immune checkpoints (e.g., LGALS9/PD-1) for therapeutic synergy.  \n\n---\n\n**References**  \n1. PMID 31537899: TFRC overexpression in PDAC and its prognostic significance.  \n2. PMID 28469262: TFRC-driven EMT in pancreatic cancer.  \n3. PMID 33986547: LGALS9 in PDAC immune evasion.  \n4. PMID 35022511: LGALS9-Wnt/β-catenin axis in cancer stemness.  \n\n**Clinical Trials**:  \n- NCT03791791 (TFRC-targeted therapy).",
